Operation Oni — Detailed Breakdown
The Scenario
You're given a compressed disk image (disk.img.gz) representing a compromised system. The attacker generated an SSH keypair on it and used the public key to authenticate to a remote server (saturn.picoctf.net).  Your job: extract the private key from the disk and use it to log in.

Step-by-Step
1. Download and decompress:

cd /tmp
wget https://artifacts.picoctf.net/c/372/disk.img.gz
gunzip disk.img.gz

2. Inspect partitions:

fdisk -l disk.img

Partition	Start Sector	Type	Size
1	2048	Linux (0x83)	100M (boot)
2	206848	Linux (0x83)	129M (root)

3. Mount the second partition (offset = 206848 × 512 = 105906176):

sudo mount -o loop,offset=105906176 disk.img /mnt

4. Locate the key:

sudo ls /mnt/root/.ssh/
# id_ed25519      id_ed25519.pub

5. Copy and fix permissions:

sudo cp /mnt/root/.ssh/id_ed25519 /tmp/key_file
chmod 600 /tmp/key_file

⚠️ Why chmod 600? SSH refuses to use a private key if it's readable by group or others. The key on the disk has permissions rw------- (600), but once you cp it as a regular user, it may end up as 644 or 664. SSH will throw:

WARNING: UNPROTECTED PRIVATE KEY FILE!
Permissions 0644 for '/tmp/key_file' are too open.

6. SSH in:

ssh -i /tmp/key_file -p 54106 ctf-player@saturn.picoctf.net

7. Grab the flag:

cat flag.txt
# picoCTF{k3y_5l3u7h_<dynamic_suffix>}

Sleuth Kit Method (no mount required)
This is the "proper" forensic approach — you never mount the image (preserving evidence integrity):

# List partitions
mmls disk.img

# List root directory of partition 2
fls -o 206848 disk.img

# Navigate to /root (inode 470)
fls -o 206848 disk.img 470
# r/r 2344: .ash_history
# d/d 3916: .ssh

# Navigate to .ssh (inode 3916)
fls -o 206848 disk.img 3916
# r/r 2345: id_ed25519
# r/r 2346: id_ed25519.pub

# Extract the private key (inode 2345)
icat -o 206848 disk.img 2345 > key_file
chmod 600 key_file

